up|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If North draws trump before playing the @HQ he will not lose a 2nd trump, though it was generally well played by leading towards the hearts twice.|
PG| ***** 18 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In 3@H East/West take four heart tricks, one diamond ruff, the @CK with @SA, and two diamond tricks for nine tricks. The question is, if West balances with 3@H, will East bid four. I don't think East should bid four with singleton @DK and three little hearts, though it is close.|
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BA Kib: ^*NWould you please review preempts by seat?|
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Sure. Let's start with 3rd seat where you should strain to preempt. If you have a weak hand, you know that 4th seat has a strong hand so preempting is most effective in 3rd seat.|
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In fact, you should sometimes preempt with even a minimum opening hand and a 6 or 7 card suit because once partner passes, chances of game are remote so preempts are good.|
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In 2nd seat, you should be relatively disciplined, although you certainly should preempt when you have a hand worth a preempt. 2nd seat is the most risky to preempt, because since dealer does not have an opener, that makes it more likely that partner has some strength and you may be preempting partner.|
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In 1st seat, you should generally favor preempting. The only time to be a little cautious is if you are vulnerable and they are not and you have a marginal preempt.|
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In summary, you should strain to preempt when you can, but just be slightly more cautious in 2nd seat, but don't be overly cautious.|
PG| ***** 25 ***** |


